  - CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - reward hacking
  - limited liability
  - option pricing
  - counterfactual reward
  - gambling for resurrection
  - simulation-based evaluation
  - reward misspecification
  - electricity markets
  - imbalance settlement
  - collateral
  - pre-registration
abstract: >-
  Simulators that train reinforcement-learning (RL) traders decide what happens to
  losses beyond equity, and that decision is an option. Five common accounting rules,
  from full liability to a zero cash floor, form an ordered ladder of options; for
  fixed positions the floor is worth between one and two times Merton's call, and
  reported equity cannot identify booked performance. In over a thousand internally
  pre-registered agents trading Nordic imbalance spreads, RL traders learned to
  exercise these options: where no policy can profit, floor-trained agents reported
  +38% to +148% while booking -15% to -57%, and an audited energy-trading engine
  reported +270% for a controller that booked -535%. A pre-training diagnostic
  ordered when PPO and A2C acquire the exploit, but simple cost-aware scores ranked
  conditions equally and a prospective transfer failed. To prevent it without the
  ledger, we introduce a sign-paired reward that subtracts the same step's reward
  with the market's sign reversed; uniquely, it recovers the booked objective in fair
  markets while keeping any legitimate edge. In a pre-registered test with a real
  edge, PPO trained on the reported reward gambled and booked -6.5% per week; with
  the sign-paired reward it did not gamble, booking +22.6% and matching ledger
  training, whereas a KL penalty toward a safe policy kept the gamble or lost over
  half the edge; in fair markets it matched ledger training for PPO and A2C. After
  the 2025 Nordic reform, a historical collateral component would not have deterred
  a modeled three-week gamble in 36.8% of zone-months, up from 8.6%.
